/P 573 0 R
/E (The emergence of Unmanned Aerial Vehicles \(UAVs\) has transitioned from a niche hobbyist interest to a cornerstone of modern industrial and defense technology. In the current era of autonomous systems, the integration of Artificial Intelligence \(AI\) with aerial platforms is no longer a luxury but a necessity for complex operations such as search and rescue, structural inspection, and dynamic surveillance. The AeroEye project is born out of this technological shift, aiming to solve one of the most persistent challenges in robotics: the seamless synchronization of high-level visual perception with low-level physical stabilization. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
703 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 573 0 R
/E (Historically, drone flight has relied on the manual dexterity of a pilot or the rigid predictability of Global Positioning System \(GPS\) waypoints. While effective in open environments, these methods fail when tasked with following a moving target in real-time or navigating through cluttered areas where human reaction speed is insufficient. AeroEye addresses these limitations by introducing a "distributed brain" architecture. By leveraging the computational power of a Ground Control Station \(GCS\) to run the YOLOv8 \(You Only Look Once\) deep learning model, the system achieves a level of spatial awareness that was previously impossible for small-scale, lightweight aircraft. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
704 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 573 0 R
/E (The technical philosophy of this project is rooted in Hardware-in-the-Loop \(HIL\) design. Most small-scale drones are powered by microcontrollers that lack the floating-point performance required to process high-definition video frames. AeroEye bypasses this hardware bottleneck by offloading the heavy "vision task" to a laptop, while the ESP32 microcontroller acts as the dedicated central nervous system for motor control. This separation of concerns ensures that the drone can maintain a high-frequency control loop making micro-adjustments to its brushless motors 50 times per second without being slowed down by the intense math required for object recognition. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
705 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 573 0 R
/E (Furthermore, the project explores the critical role of Control Theory in autonomous flight. Detecting an object is only the first half of the problem; the second half is moving toward it smoothly. AeroEye implements a sophisticated PID \(Proportional-Integral-Derivative\) control )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
