/P 574 0 R
/E (algorithm. This mathematical framework allows the system to predict target movement and adjust its momentum accordingly, preventing the "oscillatory" behavior that often leads to crashes in simpler autonomous systems. By bridging the gap between pixel-space detection and physical- space movement, AeroEye represents a scalable model for the future of intelligent, self-governing aerial robotics. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
714 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 574 0 R
/E (The "Inference-to-Action" Latency Gap In autonomous tracking, the "temporal lag" between optical target acquisition and physical kinetic response is the most common cause of catastrophic system failure. This latency is composed of three stages: frame capture latency, neural network inference time, and network transmission delay. In many existing DIY systems, the total "round-trip" delay often exceeds 150ms. In high-speed scenarios, such as tracking a walking or running human, this latency causes a "spatial mismatch" where the drone executes a correction based on where the target was, rather than where it is. This leads to a phenomenon known as "Pilot-Induced Oscillation" \(PIO\) at the algorithmic level, where violent over-corrections cause the drone to swing uncontrollably, eventually leading to a complete tracking loss or mechanical stall. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
