1820 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 652 0 R
/E (The testing phase is critical to ensure the reliability and safety of the AeroEye system. Given the high-speed nature of quadcopter flight and the complexity of real-time object detection, testing was conducted in a staged, multi-layered approach. This methodology was designed to isolate software bugs from hardware failures, ensuring that each component was "flight-certified" before being integrated into the full autonomous loop. )
/Pg 92 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1821 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 652 0 R
/E (Initial testing began in a controlled Static Bench Environment. In this phase, the quadcopter was physically secured to a weighted test stand to prevent unintended takeoff. This allowed for a high- fidelity analysis of the vision engine's ability to identify objects using the YOLOv8 model and calculate precise pixel-error offsets \(x, y\) without the risk of physical damage. We specifically analyzed "Inference Latency" measuring the time taken from the moment a frame is captured by the smartphone to the moment a command is generated by the GCS. By stress-testing the GPU with varying lighting conditions and background clutter, we confirmed that the system maintains a stable 30 FPS detection rate, which is the minimum requirement for real-time aerial tracking. )
/Pg 92 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1822 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 652 0 R
/E (Once the software logic was validated, Dynamic Testing was performed via a "Tethered Flight" configuration. The drone was attached to a safety tether that allowed for a 1.5-meter vertical range, enabling us to calibrate the PID constants \(K_p, K_i, K_d\) and power multipliers in a real-world setting. This phase was crucial for observing "Ground Effect" turbulence and its impact on the YOLOv8 bounding box stability. We observed that motor vibrations could occasionally introduce "jitter" into the camera feed; to resolve this, we implemented a Digital Low-Pass Filter in the Python control script to smooth out the error coordinates before they were transmitted to the ESP32. This resulted in a much more stable hover and prevented the "oscillatory hunting" behavior common in untuned autonomous drones. )
/Pg 92 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
