4257 0 obj
<<
/Title (Communication Bottlenecks and Protocol Overhead \(TCP vs. UDP\) The majority of standard wireless communication frameworks rely on the Transmission Control Protocol \(TCP\), which prioritizes data integrity through a "Three-Way Handshake" and packet re- transmission. However, for a real-time robotic system, a "lost" packet of data is statistically less dangerous than a "delayed" packet. The overhead and "acknowledgment" cycles of TCP introduce significant "jitter" in the motor signals, creating erratic flight behavior. Current DIY drone projects often struggle with this "Protocol Lag," where the motor speed updates cannot keep pace with the 50Hz refresh rate of the Electronic Speed Controllers \(ESCs\). A faster, "fire-and-forget" communication pipeline is required to synchronize the high-level AI "brain" with the low-level motor "muscles" in a deterministic manner.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 4255 0 R
/Next 4259 0 R
>>
endobj
4258 0 obj
<<
/Title (360-Degree Obstacle Avoidance and Environmental Mapping To ensure mission safety in complex, cluttered environments, the integration of a dedicated collision avoidance system is essential. This would involve adding an array of VL53L1X Time-of-Flight \(ToF\) laser)
/Dest [106 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 4260 0 R
/Next 4256 0 R
>>
endobj
4259 0 obj
<<
/Title (The Safety Failsafe and Communication Paradox Autonomous drones are inherently vulnerable to electromagnetic interference \(EMI\) and signal attenuation. In a system where the "intelligence" resides on an external laptop, the drone is entirely dependent on the stability of the wireless link. If the laptop running the AI crashes, or if the Wi-Fi signal drops due to distance or obstacles, most standard DIY drones are programmed to either maintain their last command \(causing a dangerous "fly-away"\) or cut power entirely \(causing a free-fall\). There is a documented lack of integrated, hardware-level "Watchdog" systems in low-cost drone projects that can instantly detect a communication blackout and transition the craft from an autonomous pursuit state into a stabilized, vertical descent mode without human intervention.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 4257 0 R
/Next 4261 0 R
>>
endobj
4260 0 obj
<<
/Title (Target Re-Identification \(Re-ID\) and Occlusion Recovery In real-world scenarios, targets are often temporarily hidden behind obstacles. Future versions will implement DeepSORT \(Simple Online and Realtime Tracking\) or similar Re-ID algorithms. This would allow the system to maintain a unique "feature fingerprint" of the target \(based on color, shape, and motion vectors\). If the subject is temporarily obscured by a tree or a building, the drone would not lose its lock; instead, it would use a predictive velocity model to anticipate the target's trajectory and re-acquire the specific individual once they reappear, preventing the system from accidentally switching to a different person in the frame.)
/Dest [106 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 4262 0 R
/Next 4258 0 R
>>
endobj
